WEBVTT

00:00:00.592 --> 00:00:23.576
<v Riley>Hey there, welcome in. On today's episode we've got the James Webb telescope finding strong evidence of an atmosphere on a scorching lava world, and permafrost melt pushing toxic metals into rivers at 70 times normal levels. We'll also dig into whether a planet's reference point can be trusted, and what the Cariboo's dark skies are really measuring. That and more, coming right up.

00:00:37.051 --> 00:00:57.819
<v Riley>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities. I'm Riley. It's Sunday, October 11th, 2026. Today's theme is Science, Wonder &amp; the Natural World. Reaching you from the heart of the Cariboo, the homeland of the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh nations.

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:23.256
<v Casey>And I'm Casey. We're looking up and out today, at telescopes, rivers, and the living systems we share the Cariboo with. Out at Horsefly Lake this morning, it's clear and around minus two. Today's high is 8 with a low of minus 2, and no precipitation is expected. Across the region, 100 Mile House is around minus 3 under clear skies, Williams Lake around minus 1, Quesnel around minus 3, and Alexis Creek around minus 2.

00:01:23.456 --> 00:01:24.288
<v Riley>On to the news.

00:01:25.688 --> 00:01:32.000
<v Riley>Most of this morning's news is in our own backyard, with the bigger science stories coming from much farther out.

00:01:33.797 --> 00:02:02.613
<v Riley>WorkBC is hosting a job fair at the 100 Mile House Community Hall, running from 11 a.m. to 4 p.m. The notice in the piece we have doesn't give a date, so confirm the day with WorkBC before you plan around it. 100 Mile House is a district municipality of about 1,900 people. In a community that size, one afternoon in one hall puts local employers and job seekers face to face, and that's a different thing from a listing online.

00:02:03.962 --> 00:02:20.834
<v Casey>The Williams Lake Royal Purple is collecting donations again to fill care bags for the local women's shelter. The bags go straight to the shelter, so the drive is only as big as what people bring in. It's a small, repeat ask. Those tend to work because the target is specific and the people asking are known.

00:02:22.236 --> 00:02:50.316
<v Riley>The Williams Lake Skateboard Association gave city council an update on a modern skatepark at the Tuesday, October 6th meeting. The design is shovel-ready, which means the plans are finished and construction can start once the money is in place. The project has now moved to a fundraising stage. Williams Lake has about 10,700 residents, so a park of this scale is a community fundraising campaign, not a line item that council can absorb quietly.

00:02:50.766 --> 00:02:54.734
<v Casey>Shovel-ready is a planning term. It says nothing about the bank account.

00:02:56.154 --> 00:03:20.202
<v Riley>The B.C. Greens have released transportation plans. They include free transit for youth and seniors, a push to develop passenger rail, and expanded ferry services. The release landed in the middle of the provincial campaign, ahead of the October 24th vote. Free transit lands differently in a rural riding depending on whether there is a route to ride. The costing is the part to read first.

00:03:20.652 --> 00:03:28.052
<v Casey>Rocky Nenka is running for mayor of the District of Wells. The Quesnel Cariboo Observer profiled him in its Meet the Candidates series.

00:03:29.623 --> 00:03:50.279
<v Riley>Advocates are urging the next B.C. government to strengthen water protection, pointing to a potentially powerful El Niño that could worsen drought across the province. My Cariboo Now carried the call. An El Niño doesn't guarantee a dry year in the Cariboo, but it shifts the odds, and the advocates want the rules in place before the season arrives.

00:03:51.436 --> 00:04:17.124
<v Casey>A disciplinary decision found that a Vernon lawyer misappropriated $116,000 from an elderly client with dementia. We covered that figure on Saturday. The Quesnel Cariboo Observer now frames it as the latest finding of professional misconduct against the same lawyer, so this is a run of decisions, not one bad day. Vernon is in the Okanagan, south and east of us. The rules meant to protect clients apply across the province, including here.

00:04:18.883 --> 00:04:54.481
<v Riley>Now the science stories, starting with a planet about as far from the Cariboo as a story can get. Earlier this week, the James Webb Space Telescope found strong evidence that HD 3167 b, a rocky lava world, has an atmosphere. The planet circles its star every 24 hours under extreme heat, and the research summary calls the discovery surprising. There is no Cariboo angle to a planet that hot. The method is the part to keep: read the light that passes through, then infer what's in the air. Nobody is sending a probe. The instrument does the work from a distance.

00:04:55.902 --> 00:05:24.382
<v Casey>ScienceAlert reports that melting permafrost is pushing toxic metals into rivers at levels reaching 70 times higher than normal. The example is the Kutuk River in Alaska's Gates of the Arctic National Park, and the story is dated September 30th, so it predates the Webb result by a week. The mechanism is thaw releasing metals that frozen ground had been holding in place. Permafrost isn't a daily fact in the Cariboo, but the principle carries. Frozen ground holds things that matter downstream.

00:05:24.831 --> 00:05:27.047
<v Riley>Seventy times. That's the number to keep.

00:05:28.796 --> 00:05:47.020
<v Riley>Outside Online reports a new study finding that wearing compression gear during long runs helps muscle recovery more than wearing it afterward. The finding is about timing, not about whether compression works at all. For runners here, that's a change to when you put the gear on, not a new gear list.

00:05:48.494 --> 00:06:11.910
<v Casey>A STAT News survey finds Americans trust science more than people in many other countries. Only 40 percent of Americans report a lot of confidence in U.S. hospitals and clinics, the same share as in Portugal and Uruguay. The headline's trust is about science in general. The hospital number is far lower, and that's the one that covers the place you go when you're sick. Those two figures don't measure the same thing.

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:15.176
<v Riley>Two questions, and the headline only answers one.

00:06:16.797 --> 00:06:40.125
<v Riley>Some of South Korea's biggest banks were hacked with help from an artificial intelligence agent. Officials say at least seven financial firms were included, and personal information for 68,000 people was stolen. The story is about the large banks. The part it leaves open is who is watching the smaller lenders and credit unions, which may run with thinner security staff.

00:06:40.575 --> 00:06:44.711
<v Casey>Big banks have security teams. The question is who's watching the small ones.

00:06:46.366 --> 00:07:07.334
<v Casey>Scientific American reports that waste from beer-making may help clean water. The research offers another way the brewing industry could reuse its by-products. The piece presents this as a new use for brewery waste, not a system that's already running. For a small brewery, the by-product question is local, because spent material has to go somewhere every single day.

00:07:08.766 --> 00:07:28.854
<v Riley>The Marginalian has a long essay on the Rashomon effect and how memories form and falter in the brain. The central idea is that recall is a reconstruction, rebuilt each time it's used. It isn't a news story in the usual sense, but it's a useful reminder to read today's list with some skepticism about what each source can actually show.

00:07:29.303 --> 00:07:35.119
<v Riley>We'll come back to the Webb result in a minute. First, a restoration operation in the Chilcotin.

00:07:36.519 --> 00:07:58.311
<v Riley>Central Chilcotin Rehabilitation Ltd. is an Indigenous joint venture doing award-winning forest rehabilitation and biomass work across Tŝideldel, Tl'etinqox, and Yunesit'in territories. The work puts crews on the land and keeps a close eye on what happens to it afterward. If you want to know who is doing restoration work in the Chilcotin, start here.

00:07:58.593 --> 00:08:02.505
<v Casey>Restoration is where the numbers meet the ground, so this one fits today.

00:08:03.905 --> 00:08:29.137
<v Riley>Roman launched August 30th at 7:26 a.m. Eastern, from Launch Complex 39A at Kennedy Space Center, aboard a SpaceX Falcon Heavy. It's heading for a Sun-Earth L2 orbit. NASA has begun commissioning, and the first test image was starlight hitting the Wide Field Instrument, taken with the detector array still stowed and far from best focus. That's an optics check, not a science result.

00:08:29.764 --> 00:08:46.572
<v Casey>The Kottke post quotes a piece saying Roman "may deliver the first direct images of distant planets in visible light." May is a forecast. Every number in today's material is a stand-in for something nobody can count directly. So when a headline reports a telescope milestone, what are we actually measuring?

00:08:46.865 --> 00:08:57.673
<v Riley>A telescope that has reached orbit and passed its first light check has crossed a real line. It's the astronomy version of checking the porch light works before you go looking for the neighbors.

00:08:58.261 --> 00:09:06.205
<v Casey>A porch light that needs a rocket and a few years to arrive. Agreed, it's a step. The planet imaging and the dark-energy work are still ahead of it.

00:09:06.835 --> 00:09:12.739
<v Riley>Then count the engineering milestones as they come. That's how a telescope reaches its science.

00:09:13.302 --> 00:09:19.702
<v Casey>Hubble and Webb both had long commissioning periods and early problems. Nobody should be surprised if Roman does too.

00:09:20.009 --> 00:09:22.953
<v Riley>Right. Long commissioning isn't failure. It's the normal path.

00:09:23.531 --> 00:09:30.203
<v Casey>Then tally it honestly. An optics check and a discovery are different entries, and only one of them goes in the headline.

00:09:30.794 --> 00:09:58.922
<v Riley>Earth is the other end of this, and the numbers get messy in a more interesting way. NASA's Space Geodesy Project tracks Earth's center of mass with satellite laser ranging, GPS, and other space and ground techniques. Earlier NASA work at JPL, using data from 1993 to 2017, already showed millimeter-scale motion. A 2026 analysis reported in ScienceDaily and Phys.org finds the annual motion is about half of previous estimates.

00:09:59.495 --> 00:10:08.367
<v Casey>Half. The new analysis combines satellite ranging with a model of how water and ice deform the crust, so part of the answer is the model. Which part changed?

00:10:08.661 --> 00:10:25.693
<v Riley>Every geodetic number has a model under it. This work doesn't say the center moves in some new way. It refines a known effect. Seasonal snow, monsoon rain, ocean water, and air mass movements shift the center by a few millimeters, and better tracking shows how much each one contributes.

00:10:26.335 --> 00:10:38.495
<v Casey>That's the part I'd want to see in the published breakdown. How much of the old figure was measurement, and how much was assumption? If an estimate gets cut in half, the thing being measured is partly the assumption.

00:10:39.095 --> 00:10:54.431
<v Riley>A revision is what sharper measurement looks like. The strongest case for the objection says the earlier number was wrong. The better answer is that it was the best the data could support at the time, and the new work uses more inputs to narrow it.

00:10:55.038 --> 00:11:07.742
<v Casey>The navigation everyone uses leans on a stand-in too. GPS and satellite orbit processing rely on a theoretical reference point for Earth's center, and the estimate of how far that center moves each year just got cut roughly in half.

00:11:08.364 --> 00:11:13.900
<v Riley>Navigation has always run on modeled numbers. A phone isn't wrong because a model got better.

00:11:14.534 --> 00:11:31.702
<v Casey>Nobody's phone is wrong. My compass app still asks me to recalibrate every time I walk past a truck. The question is whether the people who depend on that reference know it started as a model. Anybody running a survey line or a logging road with a GPS receiver trusts that reference, whether they know it or not.

00:11:32.217 --> 00:11:40.905
<v Riley>That's why the revision matters. A surveyor or a forester can't check a planetary reference by hand. They need the people doing the modeling to publish what changed.

00:11:41.488 --> 00:11:47.672
<v Casey>Then publish what changed and why. A revised number with no note about the model is a stand-in with no label.

00:11:48.343 --> 00:12:04.143
<v Riley>Now the lava world. Webb found strong evidence of an atmosphere on HD 3167 b, and that's inferred from light. Starlight passing through the planet's air tells you what's in it. The spectrum is the instrument, and the atmosphere is what you read out of it.

00:12:04.734 --> 00:12:14.462
<v Casey>So the atmosphere is itself an inference, and "surprising" tells you the expectations were wrong. The people who built the expectations are the ones reading the spectrum now.

00:12:15.033 --> 00:12:23.737
<v Riley>Being wrong about a planet is the data doing its job. Nobody expected an atmosphere on a world that hot, and the spectrum changed the expectation.

00:12:24.351 --> 00:12:33.455
<v Casey>A surprise gets reported as a discovery before the second look. I'm not calling the result wrong. The story is about a surprise, and the surprise is a model result too.

00:12:33.987 --> 00:12:40.875
<v Riley>Second looks are how it gets checked. That's the same logic as the geocenter. Revision is the process, not the failure.

00:12:40.975 --> 00:13:01.943
<v Riley>Starship is the other side of the same coin. Flight 14 became the first Starship flight to reach orbit. It deployed 26 Starlink V3 satellites at roughly 170 miles up, even though one of the six Raptor engines on the upper stage shut down early during ascent. SpaceX did not need to reuse that engine, so the mission went on.

00:13:02.607 --> 00:13:14.751
<v Casey>The mission was cut to about three hours. The plan was six laps around Earth before a planned reentry. SpaceX still has to find the cause of the engine shutdown and show that long orbital flights can be completed as planned.

00:13:15.263 --> 00:13:26.567
<v Riley>The redundancy did its job. A fault on the upper stage didn't end the flight, and the vehicle delivered operational satellites. That's the kind of iteration a reusable heavy launcher needs.

00:13:27.255 --> 00:13:37.855
<v Casey>"Largest rocket ever built" is a size measure. "Reached orbit" is a milestone. The laps are the part nobody has counted yet. If orbit is the success metric, you can declare a win and skip what failed.

00:13:38.144 --> 00:13:42.560
<v Riley>Then count the laps on the next flight. That's the purpose of a test flight.

00:13:43.100 --> 00:13:53.260
<v Casey>Fine. Count them in public, and say what didn't work. One company is carrying a large share of high-cadence launches, and the reporting we have doesn't give the root cause of the shutdown.

00:13:53.779 --> 00:13:59.659
<v Riley>Concentration is a procurement problem, not a physics problem. Right now the physics is what's being tested.

00:14:00.287 --> 00:14:10.095
<v Casey>The physics has a sky cost. Anybody at a dark rural site has seen satellite trains cross a clear sky. Nobody asked the people under those trains whether they wanted them there.

00:14:10.745 --> 00:14:19.521
<v Riley>Bring it home. Stand in a yard outside Williams Lake or Quesnel on a clear night, away from the streetlights. What is being counted there, and by whom?

00:14:19.858 --> 00:14:28.290
<v Casey>Nobody is counting aurora from downtown Quesnel. Out past town, the sky is only as good as the air over it, and summer smoke can hide an entire sky for a week.

00:14:28.803 --> 00:14:38.595
<v Riley>You can still count the aurora. Free alert apps tell you when activity is up, and a clear sky tells you the rest. That's a cheap measurement, and it's a real one.

00:14:39.147 --> 00:14:46.427
<v Casey>A clear night in August is also a smoke count. If the dark-sky number drops, you can't tell whether the sky changed or the air did.

00:14:47.063 --> 00:14:53.079
<v Riley>Then log both, and label each one. A clean-sky reading and a smoke reading are different measurements.

00:14:53.677 --> 00:15:00.341
<v Casey>Most people don't label them. Snowpack has the same problem. A snow survey samples a few points, not the whole mountain.

00:15:00.923 --> 00:15:12.235
<v Riley>A salmon count is a count at one point on a river, not the whole run. Every one of these is a proxy with a sampling method under it, and the sampling method is the part almost nobody prints.

00:15:12.866 --> 00:15:18.826
<v Casey>So who decides what the proxy is for? Is a salmon count about the fish, or about whether the fishery stays open?

00:15:19.161 --> 00:15:25.545
<v Riley>You can't answer that by making the instrument more precise. You answer it by writing down what the count is for.

00:15:26.150 --> 00:15:32.742
<v Casey>And the dark-sky argument stalls on the same problem. Smoke days need labeling, or a bad August gets read as a bad sky.

00:15:33.299 --> 00:15:46.243
<v Riley>The objection is that labeling doubles the work for volunteers, and that's real. The answer is that a two-column log costs about the same as a single column, and it stops smoke from being blamed on the lighting.

00:15:46.857 --> 00:15:52.433
<v Casey>That still doesn't answer who keeps the log in year five. Volunteer logs don't outlast their volunteers.

00:15:53.047 --> 00:16:00.799
<v Riley>Then the structural question is who owns the log. A regional body could hold it, if it publishes the protocol beside the numbers.

00:16:01.422 --> 00:16:08.598
<v Casey>I can accept that as a fix. I'm not sure a regional district has ever held a dataset this long, and I don't know the answer to that.

00:16:08.698 --> 00:16:17.410
<v Casey>Restoration crews on the ground are close to ground truth in that chain. They look at site conditions directly, not waiting for a satellite to report back on them.

00:16:17.986 --> 00:16:24.634
<v Riley>Field crews are how you check a model. A restoration program is where the stand-in and the thing itself meet.

00:16:25.190 --> 00:16:33.574
<v Casey>Only if somebody keeps the field records. Measurements drift when the person who took them leaves. A dataset without its original protocol is a list of numbers.

00:16:34.160 --> 00:16:43.624
<v Riley>The protocol has to outlast the crew. I'll give you that. Whether any agency has built that kind of protocol for the Chilcotin, I can't tell from today's sources.

00:16:44.235 --> 00:16:54.779
<v Casey>Neither can I. That's the gap. The telescope will eventually send back a planet, and the question is whether anyone will still know what each number was supposed to mean by the time it arrives.

00:16:55.340 --> 00:17:03.628
<v Riley>The question I'd put to every local body that publishes a count: who wrote down what the number stands for, and when was that note last updated?

00:17:03.964 --> 00:17:11.292
<v Casey>Ask for the sampling method next to the figure. A count with its method attached can be checked. A bare count can only be believed.

00:17:11.861 --> 00:17:22.533
<v Riley>If you have a correction, a story tip, or want to get involved, write to feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a. Tell us what you're measuring out there, and what it's standing in for.

00:17:22.821 --> 00:17:24.141
<v Casey>Hope you had a great weekend.

00:17:24.321 --> 00:17:25.561
<v Riley>Clear skies, Cariboo.